{
  "id": "lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree",
  "questionId": "lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree",
  "slug": "lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree",
  "title": "Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Search Tree",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion",
    "bst"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "in a general tree finding the LCA needs exploring both subtrees, but this tree is a BST - what extra information does that give you about where p and q must live relative to the current node's value?",
    "if both p and q are less than the current node's value, where must their LCA be? what if both are greater?",
    "if p and q fall on different sides of the current node (or one of them equals the current node's value), can the search go any further down?",
    "starting at the root, if both p and q are less than tree[i], move to 2*i+1; if both are greater, move to 2*i+2; otherwise tree[i] is the answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a general-tree LCA algorithm (exploring both children and combining results) instead of exploiting the BST property, which is unnecessary extra work",
    "using non-strict comparisons in a way that fails to stop at the current node when p or q equals it",
    "forgetting that a node counts as its own ancestor, so if p is on the path to q, p itself is the answer"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap)\nindexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node there\"\n\nThe given tree is guaranteed to be a valid binary search tree with all\ndistinct values, and you're given two values `p` and `q`, both\nguaranteed to exist somewhere in the tree. For example, `[6, 2, 8, 0, 4,\n7, 9, -101, -101, 3, 5]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n            6\n          /   \\\n         2     8\n        / \\   / \\\n       0   4 7   9\n          / \\\n         3   5\n```\n\nHere, the lowest common ancestor of `p = 3` and `q = 5` is `4` (their\ndirect parent), while the lowest common ancestor of `p = 2` and `q = 8`\nis the root, `6`.\n\n**Return the value of the lowest common ancestor of `p` and `q`: the deepest node that has both somewhere in its subtree.**\n\nA node counts as its own ancestor, so if `p` is an ancestor of `q`, the\nanswer is `p` itself.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "2 <= number of real nodes <= 10^4",
      "-100 <= tree[i] <= 100 for every real node",
      "all real node values in the tree are unique",
      "p != q, and both p and q are values that exist in the tree",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "p is a direct ancestor of q (or vice versa), where the answer is p (or q) itself",
      "p and q are the two children of the root, where the answer is the root",
      "a tree that is just two nodes"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [6, 2, 8, 0, 4, 7, 9, -101, -101, 3, 5], p = 2, q = 8",
        "output": "6",
        "expectedOutput": "6",
        "explanation": "2 is in the left subtree of 6 and 8 is in the right subtree of 6, so 6 is the deepest node containing both"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [6, 2, 8, 0, 4, 7, 9, -101, -101, 3, 5], p = 2, q = 4",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "4 is inside 2's own subtree (2's right child), so 2 is an ancestor of itself and of 4 - the lowest common ancestor is 2"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [6, 2, 8, 0, 4, 7, 9, -101, -101, 3, 5], p = 3, q = 5",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "3 and 5 are 4's left and right children respectively, so 4 is their lowest common ancestor"
      }
    ],
